{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789967006,"nonce":"h9A5Nuygm/jrIAX2yxdwCFxcRo4=","title":"Python: a parameter defaulting to None that is assigned a list is typed Object","message":"A parameter with a None default is typed by joining None with what its calls pass and what the body assigns it. None joins with a class as the class (an instance pointer may be null), but with a list as Object, so the whole body reads the parameter dynamically.\n\nReproducer (ZYNTAX_TRACE_TYPES=1 prints parse(argv: Object)):\n\n    import sys\n\n    def parse(argv=None):\n        if argv is None:\n            argv = sys.argv[1:]\n        arg = argv[0]\n        return arg.upper()\n\n    print(parse())\n\nsys.argv itself is List(Str) and a slice of it keeps the element type; the dynamic reads come from the join alone. The speed-center optparse shim's parse_args has this shape, so its `arg = argv[i]` and `arg.startswith` go through zb_any_*; it runs once per process, so the cost is start-up only.\n\nA fix is a nullable list in the type lattice (None joins with List(e) as List(e), `x is None` compares the pointer with null, every list operation stays as it is because a program that reads a None list raises in CPython too), which is a typer-wide change with its own conformance cases.","files":null}]}